Damn straight.  Anybody who doesn't make this their default assumption, and
doesn't bear it in mind any time they send information to more than a
single trusted recipient, is kidding themselves.  (And for *really*
sensitive information, remember all the systems and sysops between you and
that recipient...)

When I applied to work at AMiX, a very important part of the interview
process was a *thick* printout (50 pages of 6 point type, in two-column
landscape mode) of my posts to this list, which at that time was being
archived at Xanadu.  I did not provide this data (aside from writing it in
the first place); it came from independent records of my Extropians posts. 
I had borne in mind from the day I joined this list that what I was writing
would eventually be public knowledge.  (I was working for RAND, a defense
contractor, at the time; I knew that it was highly probable that my e-mail
was being bugged anyway -- permission to do so was a condition of my
employment and of my security clearance.)

I am certainly among those who have spoken candidly about my sex life on
the list.  However, I have done so with my eyes open.  There are things I
talk freely about, things that I can be fairly sure will not return to bite
me in the ass; and there are other things which I don't.  I never let
myself forget that there are ".mil" addresses receiving this list, and that
"joe_doakes@netcom.com" could easily be the Justice department, for all I
know.  Even before there was an Internet, there was the rule of thumb,
"Never put into writing anything you wouldn't want to see on the front page
of the newspaper with your name attached."  (Did you get a chuckle when
Michelle Peel said "Don't worry, the Enn Ess Ay is archiving the list"?  I
was half tempted to send a reply saying "No, we're not" with a fudged
"wsmith@comint.nsa.gov" return address.)

