I wouldn't call Noether's theorem (which, in this case, defines angular
momentum and relates its conservation to the invariance of the equations
of physics under rotations) nasty, convoluted and obscure.  Emmy will
be upset.

> Correct units protect against errors a lot more effectively
> than E Prime or Operationalism.

Angular momentum has units of mass*(distance)^2 / time
Magnetic moment has units of charge*(distance)^2 / time
So?

> realism is incompatible with operationalism, because realism
> assumes things that operationalism decrees to be meaningless.

How things are defined has nothing to do whether they exist.  St Anselm
made that mistake in his infamous proof of the existence of God (a god
that existed was greater than an imaginary god, he reasoned, {god being
defined as that which no greater than can be defined}, so god must
exist).  Just as the definition and existence of something are
independent, so is one's epistemological stance independent of one's
ontological stance.  Therefore operational realism is logical, non-
contradictory position to hold.

> the reason you cannot build a simple dog detection
> machine, is that ....

