From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: Anton attempts synthesis (long)

At 10:34 PM 9/28/93 -0700, D. Anton Sherwood wrote:
>
>
>There are known elements of design in many human languages.  For an extreme
>example, 

Esperanto?

[. . .]
>The possibility that the primordial human language(s) was (were) mostly
>of conscious creation is not excluded by anything known to me. 

I think your skepticism results from a desire to make too strong a distinction.

No one is saying that innovations to language, once it exists, cannot be
quite self-conscious and intentional (though whether they succeed is
another question). No one is saying that the human acts that resulted in
the birth of language were not conscious, intentional, aimed at solving a
problem. Language did not appear by magic while everyone was sleeping.

What is the other (illogical) extreme? No one seems to think that some
Neanderthal genius, frustrated at the difficulties of languageless social
life, sat down one morning and thought, "Gosh, wouldn't it be nice if we
had a language? Why don't I design one? Let's see, I'll need some nouns,
and verbs may be important, and why not throw in adjectives, adverbs, and
prepositions just for flair?" 

So, IMHO, we can exclude the extremes. What's left? Is there enough room
for two opposite sides of an argument, or just different emphases put on
the same thing? Though we can't exclude other explanations utterly, the
most parsimonious explanation of the origin of language is that it's a side
effect of intentional action.

Here's an analogy - can you have a traffic jam without designing it? Does
the fact that the cars involved in the traffic jam were designed, and the
actions of the drivers were purposeful mean that the traffic jam is a
result of design? 

Animals have proto-language (danger signals, courtship signals, etc.), who
designed these?

Perhaps language is a virus from outer space.

Dave Burns                              tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)





