My first inclination is to dismiss that statement out of hand, but
that would be committing an ad hominem fallacy.  It may actually be
applicable in some cases, though I'm not convinced that hate is ever
really useful.  Also, Christians generally mean by it "hate all
sins, love all sinners (i.e., everyone)".  For general use, I (as a
first pass) would say something more like "Be ruthless with the sins,
be compassionate towards the sinners."

My inclination is to jettison any mention of hate or sin when it comes
to myself.  Maybe some people could use them effectively, but I've
used them (the general concepts, without reference to the specific
meanings of "sin" and "sinner") to pound myself quite enough -- I
think I need to cut myself a break, and let the other people do the
being ruthless for a while.  I can always go back to self-deprecation
if I don't like thinking highly of myself.  The trick is not to think
badly of myself for not thinking badly enough of myself, which (as
ridiculous as it sounds) has been a problem when I've tried this
before.

