Date: Thu, 30 Sep 1993 0:00:41 +0100 (BST)
From: Charlie Stross <charless@sco.com>
Subject: TECH: data storage for extropians (was META: List archives.)

baumbach@atmel.com (Peter Baumbach) writes:
	
>	A small article in the back of "The Sciences" tells how we have all
>	been mislead about magnetic tape.  It seems our videos won't last more
>	than 10 years!  What media should we use?
>	
>	I told this to someone, and he told me that cdroms have a short life
>	too.  The aluminum oxidizes through the Lexan :-(  His source was
>	Rolling Stone magazine.  I don't know what issue.  
>	
>	We need gold foil cdroms.  ...or maybe these sources are wrong?

There was a controversy about this a couple of years ago. I can't cite
sources, but the upshot of the discussion (I believe according to
reports in New Scientist) was that some CD's decayed rapidly because
the ink used on the title side diffused through lexan. Some discs
were being cut with gold foil, but the eventual solution was to 
switch to non-diffusive inks. The phenomenon showed up initially
in accelerated-decay tests in which disks were stored at high temperatures
for a long period. I don't know whether anyone's yet done a large-
cohort test using large numbers of disks stored at room temperature
under routine storage conditions, to check the cumulative error rate,
but I suspect you'd find that CD's are pretty stable for time scales
in the range 10-100 years.

Certainly the best known storage methods today for long term use
are papyrus and painted/engraved stone stored in an arid climate -- but these 
were developed centuries ago; we just don't know what we can do with
today's new media, because they haven't been around long enough.

Assuming you can afford the maintenance costs, there are some very
interesting options available for long-duration storage. I'm thinking,
of course, about delay loops.

You may recall that the Apollo missions left laser reflectors on the
moon to permit accurate distance measurements. If you shine a laser
up there, about three seconds later you'll get a return pulse. Now,
that constitutes a delay line 3 light seconds long. Assuming a wavelength of
500 nm, you've got a frequency of 2Mhz; so you can fairly clearly cram
on the order of 3 Mbits between the earth and the moon. Imagine that
you've got a decoder mounted next to your laser and focussed on the mirror. 
You loop the signal back to the laser and recycle the bit stream in an 
endless loop, pumping bits up to the moon and reading them back in when
the reflected photons arrive back home. If you want to retrieve a string
of data from your delay loop, you just compare it against the input
stream until it matches.

6 Mbits in the round-trip ain't too good, but that's before we start
multiplexing frequencies. Take a thousand optical channels and you can
cram 6 Gbits of uncompressed data into that delay line. And that's just
a starting point.

The moon isn't far enough away to be much use. But it's worth noting 
that most data gets less valuable with age; indeed, the retrieval time 
that can be tolerated for old archive material is much greater than we'd
be willing to accept for that hot new marketing memo[*]. So what we need is a
delay-line storage system where the delay increases constantly. The data
capacity increases (to accomodate the growing archive) as the retrieval
performance degrades.

An interplanetary probe leaving the solar system at a speed of 30 km/sec
will add about one light second of distance per three hours -- or the
entire capacity of our earth<-->moon delay line every day.

Now I know that Galileo isn't going to be much use, following the fiasco 
with the high-gain antenna, but if NASA or J. Random Extropian ever get
arround to launching an interstellar probe, maybe they could rent out
spare bandwidth on its comms system for archive space? 

We could even store the extropians list archives in it ...



-- Charlie


[*] add smileys to taste.
Charlie Stross is charless@scol.sco.com, charlie@antipope.demon.co.uk

