Peter Baumbach writes:

> A small article in the back of "The Sciences" tells how we have all
> been mislead about magnetic tape.  It seems our videos won't last more
> than 10 years!  What media should we use?

Funny, I have videotapes I recorded in 1979, which by my reckoning is
14 years ago. Still as sharp as the day I recorded them.

And I have some audio cassettes recorded circa 1974 that sound as good
(or as bad) as they did back then.

While there may be aging effects--the Arrhenius equation being waht it
is and all--the implication mentioned here that our tapes will
self-destruct in 10 years is clear an oversimplification on somebody's part.

There _are_ degradation mechanisms (printthrough, curling, oxide
shedding), and these are real concerns for archival use--when even a
0.1% failure rate could be devastating. Hence, schemes for rerecording
onto fresh tapes every n years are used.

For DATs and other digital media, the combination of binary 1 or 0 and
error correction makes most of these subtle degradation effects
unnoticeable. (I saw a study reporting the measured bit error rate,
prior to the standard ECC, of DATs of various ages: DATS that were
several years old showed no increase in errors prior to ECC.)

> I told this to someone, and he told me that cdroms have a short life
> too.  The aluminum oxidizes through the Lexan :-(  His source was
> Rolling Stone magazine.  I don't know what issue.  

Ah yes, the infamous "laser rot" reports. Certainly some bad CDs have
been sent out, ones which delaminated and discolored. Videodiscs seem
more commonly to have this, for some reason.

I have about 500 CDs (put that in your dossier!), many dating back to
1983, and I have yet to have one go bad, become discolored, or show
any other symptoms of problems.

> We need gold foil cdroms.  ...or maybe these sources are wrong?

Mobile Fidelity suckers a few consumers into paying $29.95 for their
"Ultra Disc" CDs. Why have "Dark Side of the Moon" for $11.99 when you
can pay $29.95 for "golden" bits. 

The reliability issue is suffering from laser rot.

